<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Bucket CORS for Teren media. This is an APPLIED artefact, not a note: deploy/storage/apply-cors.sh
  puts it on the bucket, and the local rehearsal stack applies it too (minio-init in
  deploy/docker-compose.local.yml), so the rules under test are the rules that ship.

  Why it must exist at all. The phone uploads media *directly* to object storage with a presigned
  PUT (ARCHITECTURE §2, §8) — the bytes never pass through the API. That is a cross-origin request
  from the app's origin to the storage origin, so the browser sends an OPTIONS preflight first and
  refuses the PUT unless the bucket answers it. Nothing in the API can compensate for a bucket
  that does not.

  Browser CORS to storage was verified on 2026-08-29, but only against MinIO's permissive
  defaults on a laptop. That is evidence about MinIO's defaults, not about a bucket at a managed
  provider — Hetzner Object Storage starts with no CORS configuration at all, and the symptom is
  an upload that fails at the preflight with nothing in the API log, because the API was never
  called.

  __TEREN_ORIGIN__ is substituted with the app's exact origin (scheme + host + port) at apply
  time. It is deliberately not "*": a wildcard would let any page on the internet drive an upload
  with a URL it managed to obtain.
-->
<CORSConfiguration>
  <CORSRule>
    <AllowedOrigin>__TEREN_ORIGIN__</AllowedOrigin>

    <!-- PUT is the upload (§8 step 4). GET and HEAD are for the media read path that C3 still
         owes (presigned GET, ARCHITECTURE §8) — allowing them now costs nothing and means the
         bucket is not the thing blocking that increment. -->
    <AllowedMethod>PUT</AllowedMethod>
    <AllowedMethod>GET</AllowedMethod>
    <AllowedMethod>HEAD</AllowedMethod>

    <!-- The presigned PUT carries Content-Type and the SigV4 headers the SDK signed. Naming them
         individually would break the day the signing library adds one. -->
    <AllowedHeader>*</AllowedHeader>

    <!-- ETag is the only response header the upload path has any use for. -->
    <ExposeHeader>ETag</ExposeHeader>

    <MaxAgeSeconds>3000</MaxAgeSeconds>
  </CORSRule>
</CORSConfiguration>
